A.text:
Use **`Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase`**. Both functions accept it as their last argument.

```m
// List.Distinct: second argument is equationCriteria
List.Distinct({"Apple", "apple", "APPLE", "Banana"}, Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
// → {"Apple", "Banana"}   (first occurrence is kept)

// Text.Contains: third argument is comparer
Text.Contains("Hello World", "world", Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
// → true
```

In a query step:

```m
// Filter rows where [Name] contains "smith" in any casing
Table.SelectRows(Source, each Text.Contains([Name], "smith", Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase))

// Distinct values in a column, case-insensitive
List.Distinct(Source[Name], Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
```

If you need culture-aware rules (for example, accented characters or Turkish i), use `Comparer.FromCulture("en-US", true)` instead. The `true` means ignore case.

Note that `Table.Distinct` takes the same comparer as its second argument: `Table.Distinct(Source, {"Name", Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase})`.

B.text:
Use **Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase**. Both functions accept a comparer as an optional argument.

```m
// List.Distinct: the second argument is equationCriteria
List.Distinct({"a", "A", "b"}, Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
// {"a", "b"}  (keeps the first occurrence)

// Text.Contains: the third argument is comparer
Text.Contains("Hello World", "world", Comparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
// true
```

If you need culture-aware case-insensitivity, use `Comparer.FromCulture("en-US", true)` in the same position. The `true` means ignore case.
